The hlpr_join_with_aes helper function was used to merge these data, with optional normalization for gene sets (method_gs) and smoothing (smooth), including adjustment of smoothing span (smooth_span) as needed. After data preparation, we constructed a graph representing spatial relationships among spots. This was achieved by generating a positional data frame (pos) containing the x and y coordinates linked to each spot’s barcode. We then created a spatial weight matrix (weight) using the getSpatialNeighbors function from the MERINGUE package, which computes proximity between spots. To identify primary spatial patterns across features, MERINGUE calculates a spatial cross-correlation index (SCI). This index assesses the correlation between gene pairs exhibiting significant spatial heterogeneity, considering genes expressed by a sufficient fraction of cells. The SCI is determined using the following formula:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
